--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Findings
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. The Windows Phone ADSP firmware DOES expose the SLIMbus satellite control
   QMI service and DOES accept APPS satellite control.

2. The service publishes at VERSION 1, INSTANCE 0 — packing to exactly 1,
   which is what upstream's SLIMBUS_QMI_SVC_V1 / SLIMBUS_QMI_INS_ID already
   request. Upstream's exact-match lookup is CORRECT for this firmware; the
   research wildcard lookup was unnecessary.

3. Startup was triggered by LPASS SSR AFTER_POWERUP, 6 ms before the QMI
   service was discovered — the ordering in which the stock path works,
   because the ADSP was started after the driver had registered its notifier.
   The PDR (avs/audio) path and the late-registration path are therefore
   still UNPROVEN and need a cold boot with the ADSP coming up first.

4. Exactly one "SLIM controller Registered" despite two triggers firing:
   the idempotency guard behaved as intended.

5. Benign noise — the eMMC rootfs still carries .ko files for subsystems this
   build makes built-in:
     qrtr: exports duplicate symbol qrtr_endpoint_post (owned by kernel)
     qmi_helpers: exports duplicate symbol qmi_add_lookup (owned by kernel)
     qcom_common: exports duplicate symbol qcom_add_glink_subdev (owned by kernel)
